[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficients $c_i$ such that for two elements $A = e^{a^i E_i}$ and $B = e^{b^i [0m[38;2;124;124;124mE_i}$ of a Lie group, the product $AB = C = e^{c^i E_i}$ can be expressed using [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe Lie algebra structure constants.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is essentially the question of whether the group multiplication can be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrecovered from the Lie algebra — i.e., the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff (BCH) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mformula.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is: **Yes, it is possible (locally)**, via the Baker-Campbell-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHausdorff formula. The BCH formula expresses $\log(e^X e^Y)$ as a series in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mterms of iterated commutators of $X$ and $Y$, which are determined entirely by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe Lie algebra structure constants.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHowever, there are important caveats:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. The BCH series converges only in a neighborhood of the identity (local [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mresult).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Globally, the Lie algebra does not determine the group — there can be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmultiple non-isomorphic Lie groups with the same Lie algebra (e.g., $SU(2)$ and [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$SO(3)$, or $\mathbb{R}$ and $S^1$).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. The exponential map may not be surjective for all Lie groups, so not every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup element can be written as $e^X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me construct a proper proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Setup**: Let $\mathfrak{g}$ be a Lie algebra with basis $\{E_i\}$ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstructure constants $[E_i, E_j] = f_{ij}^{\;\;k} E_k$. Let $G$ be a Lie group [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwith Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$. For $A = e^{a^i E_i}$ and $B = e^{b^i E_i}$, we[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwant to find $c^i$ such that $AB = e^{c^i E_i}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**The BCH Formula**: The Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff theorem states that for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msufficiently small $X, Y \in \mathfrak{g}$:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$\log(e^X e^Y) = X + Y + \frac{1}{2}[X,Y] + \frac{1}{12}[X,[X,Y]] - \frac{1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{12}[Y,[X,Y]] + \cdots$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere the right-hand side is a series of iterated Lie brackets (commutators) of [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$X$ and $Y$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Key points of the proof**:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. **The series involves only Lie brackets**: Each term in the BCH series is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrational-coefficient linear combination of iterated commutators of $X$ and $Y$. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSince $X = a^i E_i$ and $Y = b^i E_i$, each commutator $[X, Y]$ can be expanded [0m[38;2;124;124;124musing the structure constants:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   $$[X, Y] = a^i b^j [E_i, E_j] = a^i b^j f_{ij}^{\;\;k} E_k$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   Higher iterated commutators similarly reduce to polynomials in $a^i, b^j$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwith coefficients that are rational functions of the structure constants.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2. **Convergence**: The BCH series converges absolutely for $\|X\| + \|Y\|$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msufficiently small (in any norm on $\mathfrak{g}$). This is a classical result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m3. **The coefficients $c^i$ are determined by structure constants**: Since $c^i [0m[38;2;124;124;124mE_i = \log(e^X e^Y) = \text{BCH}(X, Y)$, and each component of $\text{BCH}(X, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mY)$ in the basis $\{E_i\}$ is a convergent power series in $a^i, b^j$ whose [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficients are rational functions of the $f_{ij}^{\;\;k}$, the $c^i$ are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mindeed computable from the structure constants (locally).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m4. **Global limitations**: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   - The exponential map need not be surjective (e.g., for $SL(2, \mathbb{R})$),[0m[38;2;124;124;124mso not every $C$ can be written as $e^{c^i E_i}$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   - Even when surjective, the BCH series may not converge globally.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m   - The Lie algebra determines the group only up to local isomorphism; [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mglobally, different groups can share the same Lie algebra (e.g., $SU(2)$ vs [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$SO(3)$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write this up as a clean proof.[0m

